1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Good morning.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:03,284
Stretton Festival box office.

3
00:00:03,284 --> 00:00:06,020
How can I help you?

4
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:06,755
Oh, hello.

5
00:00:06,755 --> 00:00:10,433
My family and I are on holiday in the area,

6
00:00:10,433 --> 00:00:14,110
and we've seen some posters about the festival this week.

7
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:17,290
Could you tell me about some of the events, please?

8
00:00:17,290 --> 00:00:18,210
Of course.

9
00:00:18,210 --> 00:00:23,810
First of all, are there still tickets available for the jazz band on Saturday?

10
00:00:23,810 --> 00:00:24,753
There are,

11
00:00:24,753 --> 00:00:26,639
but only 15 punds.

12
00:00:26,639 --> 00:00:30,410
The 12 pounds seats have all been sold.

13
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:30,851
OK.

14
00:00:30,851 --> 00:00:34,380
And the venue is the school, isn't it?

15
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:34,833
Yes,

16
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:37,100
that's right, the secondary school.

17
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:41,020
Make sure you don't go to the primary school by mistake!

18
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:46,452
And there's an additional performer who isn't mentioned on the posters -Carolyn Hart is

19
00:00:46,452 --> 00:00:48,780
going to play with the band.

20
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:51,475
Oh, I think I've heard her on the radio.

21
00:00:51,475 --> 00:00:52,972
Doesn't she play the oboe,

22
00:00:52,972 --> 00:00:54,170
or flute or something?

23
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:55,314
Yes, the flute.

24
00:00:55,314 --> 00:00:57,604
She usually plays with symphony orchestras,

25
00:00:57,604 --> 00:01:01,800
and apparently this is her first time with a jazz band.

26
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,990
Well, I'd certainly like to hear her.

27
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:08,107
Then the next thing I want to ask about is the duck races -

28
00:01:08,107 --> 00:01:10,323
I saw a poster beside a river.

29
00:01:10,323 --> 00:01:11,590
What are they, exactly?

30
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:16,851
Well, you buy a yellow plastic duck - or as many as you like

31
00:01:16,851 --> 00:01:18,470
- they're a pound each.

32
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:21,180
And you write your name on each one.

33
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:26,180
There'll be several races, depending on the number of ducks taking part.

34
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:32,097
And John Stevens, a champion swimmer who lives locally, is going to start the

35
00:01:32,097 --> 00:01:32,520
races.

36
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:37,000
All the ducks will be launched into the river at the back of the

37
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,480
cinema, then they'll float along the river for 500 metres, as far as the

38
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:42,120
railway bridge.

39
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,000
And are there any prizes?

40
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,675
Yes, the first duck in each race to arrive at the finishing line wins

41
00:01:48,675 --> 00:01:53,350
its owner free tickets for the concert on the last night of the festival.

42
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:55,515
You said you can buy a duck?

43
00:01:55,515 --> 00:01:57,990
I'm sure my children will both want one.

44
00:01:57,990 --> 00:02:00,610
They're on sale at a stall in the market.

45
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:04,770
You can't miss it - it's got an enormous sign showing a couple of ducks.

46
00:02:04,770 --> 00:02:05,194
OK.

47
00:02:05,194 --> 00:02:07,315
I'll go there this afternoon.

48
00:02:07,315 --> 00:02:09,860
I remember walking past there yesterday.

49
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:13,800
Now could you tell me something about the flower show, please?

50
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:15,769
Well, admission is free,

51
00:02:15,769 --> 00:02:20,200
and the show is being held in Bythwaite Hall.

52
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,140
Sorry, how do you spell that?

53
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:26,255
B-Y-T-H-W-A-l-T-E.

54
00:02:26,255 --> 00:02:30,370
Bythwaite.

55
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:32,193
Is it easy to find?

56
00:02:32,193 --> 00:02:35,110
I'm not very familiar with the town yet.

57
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:37,316
Oh, you won't have any problem.

58
00:02:37,316 --> 00:02:39,890
It's right in the centre of Stretton.

59
00:02:39,890 --> 00:02:42,696
It's the only old building in the town,

60
00:02:42,696 --> 00:02:44,450
so it's easy to recognise.

61
00:02:44,450 --> 00:02:45,473
I know it.

62
00:02:45,473 --> 00:02:47,520
I presume it's open all day.

63
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:47,857
Yes,

64
00:02:47,857 --> 00:02:52,572
but if you'd like to see the prizes being awarded for the best flowers,

65
00:02:52,572 --> 00:02:54,930
you'll need to there at 5 o'clock.

66
00:02:54,930 --> 00:03:00,030
The prizes are being given by a famous actor Kevin Shapless.

67
00:03:00,030 --> 00:03:04,030
He lives nearby and gets involved in a lot of community events.

68
00:03:04,030 --> 00:03:05,935
Gosh, I've seen him on TV.

69
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:07,840
I'll definitely go to the prize-giving.

70
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:08,770
Right.

71
00:03:08,770 --> 00:03:13,257
I've seen a list of plays that are being performed this week,

72
00:03:13,257 --> 00:03:17,371
and I'd like to know which are suitable for my children,

73
00:03:17,371 --> 00:03:21,110
and which ones my husband and I might go to.

74
00:03:21,110 --> 00:03:22,570
How old are your children?

75
00:03:22,570 --> 00:03:23,883
Five and seven.

76
00:03:23,883 --> 00:03:26,510
What about 'The Mystery of Muldoon'?

77
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:28,860
That's aimed at five to ten-year-olds.

78
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:32,228
So if I take my children, I can expect them to enjoy it more

79
00:03:32,228 --> 00:03:32,950
than I do?

80
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:34,046
I think so.

81
00:03:34,046 --> 00:03:37,332
If you'd like something for yourself and your husband,

82
00:03:37,332 --> 00:03:42,080
and leave your children with a babysitter, you might like to see 'Fire

83
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:42,810
and Flood'

84
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:47,124
- it's about events that really happened in Stretton two hundred years ago,

85
00:03:47,124 --> 00:03:49,640
and children might find it rather frightening.

86
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:52,035
Oh, thanks for the warning.

87
00:03:52,035 --> 00:03:54,910
And finally, what about 'Silly Sailor'?

88
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:55,888
That's a comedy,

89
00:03:55,888 --> 00:03:57,844
and it's for young and old.

90
00:03:57,844 --> 00:04:02,408
In fact, it won an award in the Stretton Drama Festival a couple of

91
00:04:02,408 --> 00:04:03,060
months ago.

92
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:03,427
OK.

93
00:04:03,427 --> 00:04:04,160
Well, goodbye,

94
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:06,360
and thanks for all the information.

95
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:08,560
I'm looking forward to the festival!

96
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:09,810
Goodbye.
